ZSNES Debian packaging notes
==============================

Preparing the source tree
-------------------------

The Debian packaging files are maintained under `packaging/linux/debian/`.
When preparing a Debian source package, stage this directory as `debian/`
at the root of the source tree.

Package identity
----------------

The Debian source and binary package are named zsnes, following upstream.
The executable, desktop entries, AppStream metadata, icon and manual page
use the upstream names. Configuration paths follow upstream defaults.

Architecture
------------

Upstream ZSNES 2.3.4 completed the migration of the former 32-bit x86 assembly
implementation to C11 and now supports multiple CPU architectures.

The Debian package currently follows the Linux architectures explicitly
supported by upstream:

- amd64
- i386
- arm64
- riscv64

32-bit ARM (`armhf`) is deliberately not enabled by this packaging revision.
Upstream does not currently provide a 32-bit ARM Makefile target. armhf should
only be enabled together with a tested upstream build configuration for that
architecture.

Hardening and PIE
-----------------

The package follows upstream's architecture-specific position-independence
policy.

Upstream links i386 non-PIE. The package therefore disables PIE on i386
and retains the remaining Debian hardening options. On amd64, arm64 and
riscv64, the package retains the full hardening configuration, including
PIE.

Compression
-----------

The package builds the miniz sources bundled by upstream. ZSNES uses miniz
for DEFLATE and adds its own compatibility layer for gzip file streams.

SDL3 and audio
--------------

The package declares SDL3, PipeWire, and libao development dependencies and
explicitly enables all three backends for the build and tests.

Use `-ad pipewire` for the native PipeWire backend. Use `-ad ao:pulse` or
`-ad ao:alsa` to select the corresponding libao plugins. To compare the
SDL3 drivers, use `-ad sdl` with `SDL_AUDIO_DRIVER=pipewire`,
`SDL_AUDIO_DRIVER=pulseaudio`, or `SDL_AUDIO_DRIVER=alsa`.

See `debian/README.Debian` for the desktop launcher choices and the
PipeWire playback results on Ubuntu 24.04 (Noble).

Rendering
---------

OpenGL support remains enabled, while the SDL software renderer is also
available. A discrete GPU is therefore not a package requirement.
